# General System Description
The system is designed for deep morphological analysis of word forms (morphemic segmentation and morpheme-by-morpheme annotation). Each analyzed morpheme is assigned a two-level annotation: part-of-speech (POS) tags and standardized glosses (glosses in a meta-language).

The system acts as a "factory" for morphological analyzers based on a dictionary and rules (specifically, structural word-form patterns). These patterns can utilize both literal strings and regular expressions (using the extended regex dialect found in the Python programming language).

Object Model
Gloss ::
<form>:<pos_tags>:<gloss> [<morpheme_list>]

<morpheme_list> :: Each morpheme in this list is also a gloss, which may contain its own list of morphemes, and so on. The list of morphemes may be empty.

Example:
sì.ra:n:reste.du.souper, [sì:v:passer.la.nuit, ra:mrph:IPFV.INTR]

Pattern ::
<selection_gloss> | <marking_gloss>

<selection_gloss> :: A template gloss describing the conditions necessary to apply this rule (pattern) to a word form.

<marking_gloss> :: A template gloss that merges with the word form data ("marking" the components of the word form with morphological information).

The pattern allows for splitting the analyzed word form or morpheme into constituent morphemes. This is achieved using a special bracket notation in the <form> field of the corresponding gloss:

{<expression>|<expression>...}

<expression> :: A string or regular expression (<re>...</re>) characterizing the conditions for isolating a morpheme within the word form. The list of expressions can be of arbitrary length.

Example:
{|ma}:: -> means that a morpheme ending with the string 'ma' (e.g., 'balama') will be split into two morphemes: bala:: and ma::.

In the marking gloss, each resulting morpheme must correspond to an individual marking morpheme.

Example:
pattern :n: [ {|ma}:: ] | :n: [ :n: :mrph:COM]
Analysis example using this pattern:
balama:: -> bala.ma:n: [ bala:n: ma:mrph:COM ]

Formal Grammar Structure
The formal grammar used by the system to build a morphological analyzer is a text file containing lists of patterns (morphological rules) and directives describing the order in which patterns are applied to a word form.

The system imposes no restrictions on how rules are grouped into sections or the order of their application; the user is free to define these independently.

Grammar Sections:
Macro Definitions:
macro <string> <replacement>
Macros are simply replaced by the corresponding string throughout the grammar text to improve readability.

Analysis Order:

plan
for token:
stage <id> <add|apply> [ <lookup> | <parallel|sequential> <parse> <section_name> ]
return if parsed
Rule Sections:
section <name>
pattern <pattern>
The number of sections is unlimited. Their names are chosen by the user and subsequently used when defining the word form analysis order.

## Advantages and Disadvantages

Advantages:
+ Flexibility: The logic of the grammatical analysis (grouping and order of rule application) is entirely defined by the user in the grammar file rather than being "hard-coded" into the parser. This allows the system to adapt to languages with diverse structures.

+ Regex Support: The use of regular expressions in patterns provides extensive word-form analysis capabilities without requiring the study of proprietary tools.

+ Pattern-Based Rules: These allow for the description of specialized rules for any number of productive morphological models, increasing recognition accuracy.

Limitations:
- Rigidity: In the current implementation, the system does not allow for building complex conditional chains of rules ("if this worked, try that; if that fails, apply this to the result"). Rules are applied iteratively to the entire list of analysis results.

- Regular Grammar Constraints: While regular expressions can describe many contexts, they cannot describe all contextual conditions. Consequently, some rules may become unnecessarily complex or impossible to implement within the system.

- Specificity Issues: Pattern-based rules are less suited for non-specific models, particularly compounding (word-building), where criteria for splitting a word form cannot be formulated as string-based constraints on components. Compromises in these cases require either an extensive expansion of the rule list or abandoning non-specific models in favor of more specific ones, which can decrease recognition accuracy.